[Music]  women having trouble conceiving a child  have often reached out to fertility  clinics for help but now a new breed of  startup isn't waiting for the call these  boutique fertility operations are  encouraging clients in their 20s and 30s  to freeze their eggs to preserve the  prospect of a future pregnancy not  everyone is convinced this full profit  outreach is appropriate though many  clients are grateful they took up the  offer I've always known that I want kids  like I always like always tell everyone  I'm destined to be a mom  so when model Christa Mays found herself  single in her early 30s with the goal of  being a mom by age 35 she decided she'd  have to find a less traditional approach  than most to motherhood  I was having a conversation with my  girlfriend's about egg freezing and  fertility and the conversation I had was  like I think I'm gonna do this I don't  know anything about it I don't know what  office is to go to like how do I look up  on the computer fertility clinic that  wants somebody like me she came across  ads for kind body on Instagram the  fertility startup known for these bright  yellow vans offering blood tests to  measure the anti-mullerian hormone AMH  which is thought to be a marker of a  woman's remaining egg supply when I  found out that my MH was at 0.78 which  is low I basically just kind of said  okay what do I have to do  let's get your blood drawn okay Mays  went to have more testing done at kind  bodies New York City clinic that's when  she decided to freeze her eggs what was  it like he just laid down on the bed and  then your anesthesiologist just you know  get your IV going and then before you  know you wake up doctors were able to  retrieve seven eggs and with them the  hopes of a baby I'm feeling pretty good  just woke up from anesthesia you've  slowed your biological clock in a way or  you've taken your biological clock out  of the mix yeah yeah so I mean my eggs  are you know they'll be 3232 forever so  for me it was taking the opportunity to  ensure that I'll have children  regardless of my relationship situation  kind body is one of a growing crop of  companies suggesting young women protect  themselves against future infertility  you don't do anything about fertility  unless you're having a fertility problem  and we want to change that founding  physician dr. Fahy misison talks to a  packed room of young women at monthly  egg freezing 101 events and says ads  like this one which say freezing eggs is  like freezing time are not misleading if  your natural fertility is going to start  to decline or in your mid-thirties by  freezing your eggs you're giving  yourself the gift of time egg freezing  is not a guarantee to motherhood however  in a woman who is having difficulty  conceiving if she happens to have eggs  that are from her younger healthier  better quality version of herself it  will be easier for her to get pregnant  than if she who is having fertility  issues tries to use her own eggs at that  time according to the American Society  for Reproductive Medicine the rate of  women between the ages of 40 to 44  becoming first-time moms has been rising  since the early 80s the reasons include  careers a less than ideal relationship  status or finances to women in their 20s  will really need to be concerned with  their fertility so concerning means I'm  trying to scare you into something and  that's definitely not the process I want  you to be aware of it because I think  that knowledge is power that knowledge  bringing it to those 20 year olds allows  them to make a decision that's right for  them Gina Bart AZ is the founder and CEO  of kind body at about six thousand  dollars she says one egg freezing cycle  here not including medication costs far  less than at some major fertility  centers and could mean more savings if a  woman requires in vitro fertilization  later if you measure the cost of having  a  with your frozen eggs and you compare it  to multiple failed rounds of IVF every  single time the one cycle with those  frozen eggs yields a better outcome and  a more affordable solution but not  everyone is on board if there are issues  a reproductive endocrinologist should be  the person they should be providing all  this information to women and not  entrepreneurs dr. Pasquale Patricio is  the director at the Yale fertility  Center he worries about startups baiting  young women with flashy ads and AMH  testing for the great overwhelming  majority of women in their young age  there is no reason to offer single test  that by itself is completely inefficient  and only generates fear and panic but  dr. suzanne says kind body makes this  clear the initial tests in the yellow  van is not meant to be diagnostic AMH is  a piece of the puzzle some of the other  pieces are one just going over your  medical history and then a antral  follicle count which is a special type  of pelvic ultrasound or sonogram where  we can actually count the number of  follicles which are basically premature  eggs there isn't much data on the  long-term safety and effectiveness of  planned egg freezing especially when it  comes to eggs that were flash-frozen  using a relatively new technique called  vitrification the eggs are cooled an  ultra rapid rate to negative 196 degrees  Celsius 1 2017 studies suggested a 34  year old woman with 20 frozen eggs is  expected to have a 90% chance of having  at least one live birth a 42 year old is  expected to have a 37% chance  Crysta Mays says she's likely to go  through two more rounds of egg freezing  because she's hoping for more than one  child my goal that I've talked to you  about with my doctors is freezing twenty  eggs it is an investment but I felt like  it's it's a justified investment and an  important one to you yeah this is my  backup plan if I don't have a partner  and I can't do it the natural way in the  freeway my next option would be  to do this my biggest takeaway from  talking with all these women and some  other young woman is they want to know  their options and they're just not  hearing this from their doctors that  this is an option it's expensive but  what they're hoping at kind body is the  more that start to do this more  insurance companies will get on board  and that changes things super expensive  insurance policy but 25 years ago this  was not available to a lot of women and  you know availability opportunity always  a good thing it's a lot of sense

